# Build context for the outreach service is Python/ itself (see
# linkedinmcp/Dockerfile and linkedinmcp/deploy.cmd, both of which build from
# here with `-f linkedinmcp/Dockerfile`). Keep that context small, and -- more
# importantly than size -- keep the one credential out of every layer that must
# never be in one.

# The Firestore service-account key. Cloud Run authenticates as the runtime
# service account through Application Default Credentials, so this file is
# not merely unnecessary in the image, it is a credential that must never be
# baked into a layer. Only one copy exists in the tree today (Python/), but
# this is the one line in this file where "any depth" is the right answer,
# not just the context root -- unlike the .env line below, a stray nested
# copy must not slip through, so this keeps the `**/` prefix even though the
# brief that specified this file showed it without one.
**/*service-account.json

# NOT excluded: .env. The container has no other source of Unipile and Gemini
# credentials, so linkedinmcp/Dockerfile copies Python/.env in deliberately and
# the service reads the same configuration the notebooks do. linkedinmcp/.env,
# if it exists, ships too and layers overrides on top. Adding `.env`, `*.env` or
# `**/.env` here would fail the build at the Dockerfile's `COPY .env`, and an
# image without the file would refuse to start anyway: `OUTREACH_API_KEY` lives
# there too.
#
# The consequence is that **the image contains real secrets**: anyone with read
# access to the Artifact Registry repository can pull it and extract them. That
# is the accepted trade for a one-command deploy.

.venv/

# A bare, slash-less pattern in .dockerignore matches only at the context
# root (see the .env comment above) -- so plain `__pycache__/` and `*.pyc`
# would catch Python/__pycache__ and miss linkedinmcp/__pycache__,
# lib/__pycache__, lib/unipile/__pycache__ and lib/unipile/resources/__pycache__,
# all of which exist in this tree right now and would otherwise ride into the
# image via `COPY lib/ lib/` and `COPY linkedinmcp/ linkedinmcp/`. The `**/`
# prefix matches at every depth.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc

.git/
.superpowers/
*.ipynb
*.csv
*.txt
tests/

# Do NOT add *.md here. pyproject.toml declares `readme = "README.md"`, and
# hatchling reads that file to build the project's metadata; `uv sync` (see
# linkedinmcp/Dockerfile) installs this project, not just its dependencies, so a
# missing README.md fails the build with
# "OSError: Readme file does not exist: README.md". This already happened
# once on Dockerfile.deploy -- keep README.md shipping into the context.
